  "card_sha256": "26bc47fc8c618869d69fcc65072a928068ee8775022865d24a7fdec9c5df94c0",
  "title": "Maximum-rate stratification gives a finite-bit approximation to rare product-chain hitting times",
  "topics": [
    "probability",
    "analysis",
    "complexity",
    "acquisition",
    "methods"
  ],
  "claim_status": "source_derived_unreviewed",
  "reported_status": "The later CN packet adds a source-reported exact-integer/rational finite-bit implementation for dyadic inputs, an internal certificate and code audit, a deterministic same-tape enclosure, a q=2^-200 bounded fixture, one OS-backed acquisition with raw prefix replay, and a 17-test receipt. For the specified n=4 instance the source states a conservative coupling-failure bound 59429/5242880 under independent unbiased bits. The tape-specific relative discrepancy upper bound is approximately 0.001652145 for every extension of the accepted cells; it is not a statistical error-rate estimate.",
  "scientific_scope_status": "This is implementation-level evidence for selected dyadic inputs under the N592 stationary product initial law and one joint target. The broader N592 rational-input theorem remains source_derived_unreviewed. CN does not close its independent proof gate, certify the bit source, strengthen the error metric, establish physical acquisition or novelty, or change the mathematical claim status.",
  "status_authority": "N592 and its original CK mathematical specification remain the authority for the general theorem candidate. CN supplies a separate immutable implementation packet and only supports the concrete dyadic implementation scope stated there. The N592 proof-reconstruction gate remains open.",
